yed aggressively in most situations.\n\nLet me analyze my options:\n1. **Fold (-1)**: This would be a terrible decision with such a strong hand. A-K has excellent equity against most hands.\n\n2. **Check (0)**: My opponent just checked (action 0), so I could check back and see the flop for free. However, this is passive and doesn't build the pot when I have a strong hand. It also doesn't extract value or protect my hand.\n\n3. **Raise (≥200)**: This is the most strategically sound option. A-K plays better in a raised pot because:\n   - It builds the pot when I likely have the best hand\n   - It may get worse hands to fold, improving my win probability\n   - It defines my opponent's hand range better based on their response\n   - A-K benefits from reducing the number of opponents (heads-up is ideal)\n\n4. **All-in (-2)**: Too aggressive for this stage. With 19,900 chips and the pot being small, an all-in is an overbet that would only get called by premium hands that have me in trouble (AA, KK) or dominated (like AK suited). This wastes fold equity and doesn't maximize value.\n\nSince this is hand 1 of 20 and we're essentially even (I'm down only 100 chips from the blinds), I should play standard aggressive poker. A standard raise of 3x the big blind would be 300 chips (assuming 100 BB structure), but 200 is the minimum raise allowed, which suggests the big blind is 100.\n\nReason:\nI'm holding A-K off-suit, a premium starting hand that performs well against most opponent ranges. My opponent checked, showing weakness or a trapping strategy. A raise accomplishes multiple goals: building the pot with a strong hand, gaining information about my opponent's holding, and potentially winning the pot immediately if they fold. Given it's early in the match with plenty of chips to play, a modest raise of 200 chips is appropriate to maintain fold equity while not overcommitting.\n\nAnswer: 200"}
