00 chips\n- Hand 19/20 - second to last hand\n- Leading by 2,700 cumulative chips\n\n**My Hand:** K♦ 10♠\nThis is a decent but not premium hand. It's a playable hand in heads-up poker:\n- King-high with a reasonable kicker\n- Can make top pair\n- Potential for straights\n- Not strong enough to go all-in pre-flop typically\n\n**Opponent's Action:** Checked (called the blind)\n- Shows they don't have a premium hand worth raising\n- Could have anything from weak to medium strength\n- Checking gives me initiative\n\n**Strategic Considerations:**\n1. **Raise (200+):** With K-10, a standard raise would apply pressure and build the pot when I likely have the better hand. In heads-up play, K-10 is above average. A raise of 200-400 chips would be reasonable.\n\n2. **Check (0):** See a free flop. However, this gives up initiative and allows opponent to see cheap cards with any two cards. Against a random hand, K-10 is favored but not dominant.\n\n3. **All-in (-2):** Too aggressive for K-10 pre-flop. I'm already leading and don't need to risk everything on a marginal hand.\n\n4. **Fold (-1):** Too weak - K-10 is playable in heads-up and I'm getting good pot odds.\n\nGiven this is hand 19/20 and I'm leading, I should play solid poker. K-10 is strong enough in heads-up to raise and apply pressure. A modest raise around 200-300 chips builds the pot while giving me fold equity if opponent has weak cards.\n\nReason:\nK-10 offsuit is above average in heads-up play. Since my opponent only checked, they likely don't have a premium hand. Raising here accomplishes multiple goals: (1) builds the pot when I likely have the best hand, (2) takes initiative in the hand, (3) might win immediately if opponent folds, (4) doesn't risk my chip lead unnecessarily. A raise of 200 chips is the minimum and represents a standard 2x big blind raise.\n\nAnswer: 200"}
